If `key` is present, replaces its value with `f(value)` (which borrows the old value);
otherwise inserts `init` (dropped unused when `key` is present). One lookup either way:
`m.upsert(word, 1, (n) => n + 1)` counts words.

Returns the `[key, value]` pairs for every entry in the map, in insertion order (an array;
JS returns an iterator). `for (const [k, v] of m.entries())` and `for (const [k, v] of m)`
iterate live, as `keys()` does.

A fence: with `"acquire"`, later reads and writes of this thread stay after the reads before
it; with `"release"`, earlier ones stay before the writes after it; `"acqrel"` both;
`"seqcst"` also in the one order of all `seqcst` operations.

A recursive CTE: `initial UNION [ALL] recursive`. Rows go through a queue: each row taken
from it is output, then the recursive part runs with the CTE standing for that row alone,
and its rows join the queue (UNION drops rows seen before), as SQLite runs them.

exports.doc:
How many workers run tasks: fixed for the process (`VELT_THREADS`, else the number of cores;
1 on WebAssembly), with either scheduler. Calling it doesn't change the scheduler: only
pinning a task (`spawn(p, { worker })`) selects the per-core one.
